#ifndef EWOK_STL_CSIGNAL_H
#define EWOK_STL_CSIGNAL_H

/*
 * <csignal> - the C <signal.h> names, in namespace std.
 *
 * Read the caveat before using any of this, because on EwokOS the interface is
 * far wider than the mechanism underneath it.
 *
 * The kernel delivers exactly two signals: SYS_SIG_STOP (0) and SYS_SIG_KILL
 * (1).  <signal.h> defines the whole familiar POSIX name set anyway - SIGINT,
 * SIGTERM, SIGSEGV, SIGPIPE, SIGCHLD, SIGWINCH and the rest - but gives them
 * numbers at or above SYS_SIG_NUM, and signal()/sigaction() treat those numbers
 * as invalid.  Registering a handler for SIGINT therefore compiles, links, and
 * quietly does nothing; the handler is never called because nothing ever raises
 * it.  That is a deliberate design in libc's header, documented there, and it
 * exists so ported code (libcurses job control, window-resize handling, the
 * usual SIGPIPE-ignoring boilerplate) builds and runs rather than failing to
 * compile.  It is not a working signal system, and code whose correctness
 * depends on actually receiving one needs a different mechanism - an IPC
 * message, or a file descriptor watched through select().
 *
 * SIGTERM and SIGKILL are both mapped to SYS_SIG_KILL, so raising either really
 * does terminate the process.  Those two names are the ones that mean something.
 *
 * sig_atomic_t comes from libc rather than being defined here; see the comment
 * on its typedef in <signal.h> for why int satisfies the guarantee on every
 * architecture this tree targets.
 *
 * The macros - SIG_DFL, SIG_IGN, SIG_ERR, SIG_HOLD, SIG_BLOCK, SIG_UNBLOCK,
 * SIG_SETMASK, SA_RESTART, SA_INTERRUPT, SA_RESETHAND and every SIGxxx number -
 * need no using-declaration, being macros.
 */

#include <signal.h>

namespace std {

/* ISO C: the two functions and the one type that [csignal.syn] requires. */
using ::sig_atomic_t;
using ::signal;
using ::raise;

/* Types libc provides beyond the ISO set.  sighandler_t is the shape of a
   handler as signal() takes and returns it; sigset_t is a plain unsigned long
   here rather than a bitmask struct, which is consistent with there being two
   real signals. */
using ::sighandler_t;
using ::sigset_t;

/* The POSIX surface.  All of it is inert for the reasons described above except
   kill() and raise(), which reach the kernel's two real signals. */
using ::kill;
using ::sigemptyset;
using ::sigfillset;
using ::sigaddset;
using ::sigdelset;
using ::sigismember;
using ::sigprocmask;
using ::sigaction;

/* `using ::sigaction` exports the FUNCTION only, and that is worth stating
   plainly because the opposite looks like it ought to be true.  At global scope
   <signal.h> declares both `struct sigaction` and `int sigaction(int, const
   struct sigaction*, struct sigaction*)`; in C++ the function name hides the
   class name, so an unqualified use picks the function and a using-declaration
   carries across just that one.  Attempting to name the type through the
   alias is a compile error, not a subtlety:

       std::sigaction sa;          // error: expected ';' before 'sa'
                                   // warning: statement is a reference, not
                                   //          call, to function 'sigaction'
       struct sigaction sa;        // correct - elaborated type specifier

   Nothing is lost by it.  sigaction is POSIX, not ISO C, so [csignal.syn] does
   not ask for either entity, and the struct is reachable from C++ exactly as it
   is on glibc - by spelling `struct`.  A using-declaration cannot name a hidden
   class, and there is no spelling that would make one do so. */

}

#endif // EWOK_STL_CSIGNAL_H
